module Darklang.Cli.Docs.Traits

let content () : String =
  """# Traits

A trait names a set of fns over one open type. An impl says how one type does
them. A call picks the impl by the type of the value it is given.

## Declare
  trait Show<'a> =
    let show (value: 'a) : String

  A trait is a package item like a type or a fn: `dark view Stdlib.Add` prints
  it, `dark ls Stdlib` lists it under Traits. Two traits with the same methods
  and shapes are the same trait, whatever they are named, so do not redeclare
  `Add`, `Ord` and friends: they exist, and `impl Add for X` finds them.

## Implement
  impl Show for Point =
    let show (p: Point) : String = $"({p.x}, {p.y})"

  impl Show for Int64 =
    let show = Stdlib.Int64.toString      # an existing fn, no wrapper made

  impl<'a: Show> Show for List<'a> =     # conditional: needs Show for the element
    let show (xs: List<'a>) : String = ...

  The implementation is named <Module>.<Type>.<Trait> (`Acme.Point.Show`) and
  its method fns live beneath it (`Acme.Point.Show.show`). `dark impls Show`
  lists a trait's implementations; `dark ls Acme.Point` shows them under
  Implementations.

## Author from the shell
  dark trait /Acme.Show 'let show (v: '"'"'a) : String'
  dark impl /Acme 'Show for Point = let show (p: Point) : String = ...'
  dark module Acme - <<EOF ... EOF            # several at once, with the type

  Like `dark fn` and `dark type`: the path says where, the text is the
  declaration without its keyword. An impl's path is the module it is written
  in; the item lands at <Module>.<Type>.<Trait>.

## Call
  Show.show p            # dispatch on p's type
  p.show                 # same, when p has no field `show` (a field always wins)
  a.add b                # a two-argument method as a receiver call

## Bound a type parameter
  let display<'a: Show> (v: 'a) : String = "value: " ++ Show.show v
  let sum<'a: Add + Zero> ...            # several bounds join with +

  A bound is checked when the fn is ENTERED, against the caller's actual type,
  not when the method is reached. The checker checks it statically too:
  `MissingImpl`, `AmbiguousImpl`, `UnboundTypeParameter`.

## The operators are traits
  `a + b` is `Stdlib.Add.add a b`; `- * / % **` are Sub, Mul, Div, Mod, Pow; `-x`
  is Neg; the comparisons are `Stdlib.Ord`. Every builtin numeric type implements
  them, so `Vec + Vec` only needs `impl Add for Vec`, and `Stdlib.max`, `min` and
  `clamp` work on anything with an `Ord`. `"a" + "b"` is `"ab"` (String has an
  `Add`; `++` still works); `"a" - "b"` is a missing implementation, not a
  numeric-type error. `++` and the bitwise operators are not traits.

## Equality, rendering, identities
  `a == b` is `Stdlib.Eq.equals a b` (`!=` is its negation). Every value
  compares structurally unless its type implements `Eq`, so nothing changes
  until you write `impl Eq for User = let equals (a: User) (b: User) : Bool =
  a.id == b.id`; then `==` on two Users is yours. Builtin types are never
  overridden, and `List.sort` and dict keys stay structural. Inside a list or
  a record the walk is structural too: `[u1] == [u2]` does not consult `Eq`.

  `ToString` is the trait behind every builtin type's `toString`: `(42L).toString`,
  `p.toString` once `impl ToString for Point` exists, `Stdlib.toString v` for
  any type with one. `Stdlib.toRepr` stays the structural debug form.

  `Zero` and `One` are the identities: `Zero.zero<Int64> ()`, or from a bound
  inside a generic fn. `Stdlib.sum seed xs` and `Stdlib.product seed xs` take
  the seed as an argument so `[]` never has to ask which type it is.

## Visibility
  An implementation is a package item like any other: it is visible where its
  name is bound (main, or your branch). Two visible implementations of one
  trait for one type is an ambiguity: the call says so, and `dark constraints`
  keeps it as a finding until you deprecate one (a deprecated implementation is
  not dispatched to) or `ack` it.

## Errors
  No implementation of Acme.Show for Float   # write `impl Show for Float = ...`
  More than one implementation of ...        # `dark impls Show` lists them
  Cannot tell the type ... dispatches on # the self argument's type is unknown;
                                         # give an explicit type arg or a bound"""
